Check the declared content size when a frame ends in an empty last block.

ZSTD_decompressContinue(), the streaming path, compares the bytes a frame
decoded with its Frame_Content_Size at the end of a non-empty last block
(ZSTDds_decompressLastBlock). A last block of size 0 never reaches that
stage: ZSTDds_decodeBlockHeader ends the frame directly, with no check. The
one-shot ZSTD_decompressFrame() checks after every last block, so the same
frame -- one that declares a content size of 4312 and holds a single empty
raw block -- was refused when decoded in one call and accepted, as an empty
frame, when streamed with less output room than its declared size (zukomp's
fuzz oracle comparing a split decode with a whole one, #68).

The check added is the existing one, moved to the other place a frame can
end. Written to be upstreamable as-is.

--- a/decompress/zstd_decompress.c
+++ b/decompress/zstd_decompress.c
@@ -1322,6 +1322,15 @@
             }
             /* empty block */
             if (bp.lastBlock) {
+                /* The end of the frame, so the same content-size check as
+                 * a non-empty last block gets below and the one-shot path
+                 * makes in ZSTD_decompressFrame(). Without it a frame that
+                 * declares a content size and ends in an empty last block
+                 * was accepted whatever it had decoded. */
+                RETURN_ERROR_IF(
+                    dctx->fParams.frameContentSize != ZSTD_CONTENTSIZE_UNKNOWN
+                 && dctx->decodedSize != dctx->fParams.frameContentSize,
+                    corruption_detected, "");
                 if (dctx->fParams.checksumFlag) {
                     dctx->expected = 4;
                     dctx->stage = ZSTDds_checkChecksum;
